Subject: [PATCH 0/8] sparc64: fix MSI queue initialization and teardown

These eight patches fix shared sparc64 MSI initialization, dispatch and
teardown paths used by Fire and sun4v PCI. They apply to vanilla v7.3-rc1
and do not depend on M3000 support or the separate ULi PCI quirk.

Clear MSI descriptor associations before freeing software IRQs, publish
dispatch state before registering queue handlers, retain hardware-published
storage on failed initialization, and synchronize consumers on teardown.
Check hardware invalidation, quarantine failed message IDs, and register
handlers before enabling queues, preserving sun4v's activation order.

Validate queue heads and MSI numbers before accessing arrays or hardware
state. Consume out-of-range records without dispatch, retain progress
after wrap/error, and keep the existing drain-until-empty behavior.

On an Ultra45, the pre-follow-up kernel warned in msi_domain_free_descs()
when closing the spare tg3 NIC. The combined follow-up did not warn over
three teardown/reallocation cycles. Register, memory, internal loopback
and interrupt tests passed; NVRAM/link failures were also present in the
baseline and external loopback was not requested. Network transfers
passed checksum comparisons. This is combined-series comparison evidence,
not a bisection of the descriptor fix alone.

The same machine exposed an invalid MSI record of 257 with only 256
allocated entries. The old source uses the value as an unchecked table
index and Fire register offset. This demonstrates a concrete invalid-
access path; actual memory corruption or wrong-IRQ dispatch was not traced.
A ULi EHCI function reads back programmed MSI1 as 0x0101. Its separate
no-MSI quirk restored working USB control and bulk reads on this machine.
That quirk is not a prerequisite for validating and discarding bad records.

Testing and limits
------------------
On a T5120 primary domain with eight vCPUs and 8GB RAM, vanilla v7.3-rc1
and vanilla plus these eight patches were booted from RAM-only network
images. Both used the same configuration except LOCALVERSION, with block
drivers and modules disabled. No M3000 patches or PCI quirks were included.
The Intel 82571 NET0 device used e1000e and native pci_sun4v MSI at 1Gb/s;
MSI mode and interrupt delivery were checked in sysfs and /proc/interrupts.

Vanilla warned in msi_domain_free_descs() on the first interface open,
through pci_disable_msi() and e1000e_reset_interrupt_capability(), and
became tainted. With this series, the same sequence produced no warning
and the kernel remained untainted. This compares the complete eight-patch
series with vanilla; the warning was not bisected to an individual patch.

Both kernels passed an 8MiB download and upload, three link down/up
cycles, and three driver unbind/bind cycles. Each rebind was followed by
another 8MiB download. All transfer hashes matched, MSI interrupts were
delivered, and final NIC error/drop counters were zero. No throughput
improvement is claimed.

Ultra45 coverage uses the combined follow-up described above, whose final
source is unchanged by the split into independent series. Its final kernel
was untainted and networking used MSI. This is Fire UP coverage, not Fire
SMP coverage or an independent eight-patch-only Ultra45 boot.

The T5120 tests exercised MSI on one physical Ethernet function, not
MSI-X. Allocation or hypervisor failures, queue overflow, pending-record
races and all quarantine/reuse paths were not exercised on hardware.
Mocks cover failure paths and queue handling, not hardware completion.
Unknown record types retain the existing error path. Safe message-ID reuse
still relies on existing hardware assumptions; these patches do not prove
a hardware drain protocol or solve continuous-input handler fairness.

Patch 1 is self-contained and can be applied ahead of patches 2-8. Its
runtime evidence above compares the full series, not patch 1 alone.

Patches 1 and 6 request stable consideration for the observed bugs.
Patch 6 requires patch 2's dispatch initialization changes or an equivalent
adaptation; that pair applies to v7.3-rc1 without the other patches.
Patches 2-4 retain Fixes tags but no automatic stable request. Older stable
targets still need backport review and testing; patch 6 combines bounds
checks, ordering and progress handling.

All eight commits passed full sparc64 UP and SMP W=1 vmlinux builds
on the vanilla base. Independent git am reproduces the exported tree.

Codex assisted with implementation, source review and test preparation.

